---
title: "80 Wooster Street: A Share in SoHo"
authors: [Jay Son]
broadcast: 4
order: 3
---

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-01.jpg" alt="Google map of SoHo with a pin at 80 Wooster Street, between Spring and Broome Streets" pie="Location of 80 Wooster Street" /%}

In April 2021, a third-floor loft at 80 Wooster Street appeared on the market for just under $3 million. Whitewashed brick walls, original cast-iron columns, and eleven-foot ceilings framed a 2,400-square-foot apartment configured with a single bedroom.{% ref n=1 /%} The listing promised a "breathtaking artist's loft."{% ref n=2 /%} CityRealty's building overview supplied the pedigree, naming Fluxus founder George Maciunas and describing the building's history as "inseparable from SoHo's early loft culture."{% ref n=3 /%}

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-02.jpg" alt="Kitchen with wooden counters, hanging pots and warm light in a loft at 80 Wooster Street" pie="Kitchen in Caroline and Rod Keating's third-floor loft, 2010" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-03.jpg" alt="Listing photograph of a whitewashed loft with cast-iron columns, white sofas and a chandelier" pie="Listing image of 80 Wooster Street #3R, 2021" /%}
{% /galeria %}

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-04.jpg" alt="Screenshot of the StreetEasy listing description for 80 Wooster Street #3R, with phrases highlighted in yellow, among them SoHo's original co-op and the income from the commercial tenant The RealReal" pie="Listing details of #3R in 80 Wooster Street" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-05.jpg" alt="Screenshot of CityRealty's overview of 80 Wooster Street, with highlighted phrases naming Fluxus founder George Maciunas and Manhattan's first artist co-op" pie="Listing details of #3R in 80 Wooster Street" /%}
{% /galeria %}

More than fifty years earlier, the entire building had sold for $105,000. In 1967, Maciunas organized the former cardboard factory as Fluxhouse Cooperative II, an artists' cooperative. A foundation loan and prospective occupants' deposits supplied the down payment, with the seller carrying the remaining mortgage.{% ref n=4 /%} Among its early occupants were choreographer Trisha Brown, sculptor Charles Ross, and Fluxus artist Robert Watts. Downstairs, filmmaker Jonas Mekas established the Film-Makers' Cinematheque, accommodating experimental screenings and performances beneath the artists' homes and studios.{% ref n=5 /%}

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-06.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of the cast-iron facade of 80 Wooster Street seen from the street in the 1940s" pie="Exterior of 80 Wooster Street, ca. 1945" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-07.jpg" alt="Loft interior with a wooden office partition, a television and tall windows onto the street" pie="Bill Downey's second-floor front loft with original Miller Cardboard Company office partition, 2010" /%}
{% /galeria %}

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-08.jpg" alt="Typed and hand-annotated page of the Fluxus Manifesto" pie="Fluxus Manifesto, 1963" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-09.jpg" alt="Typewritten Fluxhouse newsletter page with tables of costs and floor assignments" pie="Fluxhouse Newsletter No. 10, February 8, 1967" /%}
{% /galeria %}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-10.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of a man in a harness walking down the brick facade of 80 Wooster Street past the fire escape" pie="Joseph Schlichter Performing “Man Walking Down the Side of a Building” by Trisha Brown at 80 Wooster St., ca. 1970" /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-11.jpg" alt="Rainbow spectrum cast across the floor of a dark loft by a prism in the window" pie="Charles Ross' prism sculpture Spectrum, in his fourth-floor rear loft" /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-12.jpg" alt="Printed flyer with an engraved head pierced by lines, announcing the S.F. Mime Troupe at the Film-Makers' Cinematheque, 80 Wooster Street" pie="Flyer for opening performance at Cinematheque by the San Francisco Mime Troupe, ca. 1967" /%}

In their 2010 book *Illegal Living: 80 Wooster Street and the Evolution of SoHo*, Roslyn Bernstein and architect Shael Shapiro, himself an early resident, trace the building's history and its occupants' changing fortunes. Buying into the cooperative meant financing its conversion. Maciunas's proposed renovations to 80 Wooster exceeded the building's purchase price, but banks would not lend for a venture where residential occupation was prohibited. Some prospective buyers abandoned their plans when they could not meet the payments, and others borrowed from relatives.{% ref n=6 /%} Many lived for years in a state of perpetual construction: early residents Linda and Joel McGlasson carried water upstairs from the basement bathrooms. Ownership gave residents a stake in the building, but making it habitable remained their expense.{% ref n=7 /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-13.jpg" alt="Flyer comparing the sale prices, renovation costs and floor plans of Fluxhouses No. 1 to 4, with the renovation note highlighted in yellow" pie="Flyer with early floor plans of three Fluxhouses" /%}

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-14.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of a bare loft with a toilet standing in the open and debris on the floor" pie="A loft in move-in condition, ca. 1970" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-15.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of a loft studio with stretcher frames, a ladder and a cluttered worktable" pie="Studio area of the Lewandowskis' fifth-floor rear loft, ca. 1970" /%}
{% /galeria %}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-16.jpg" alt="Front page of the SoHo Newsletter with the headline SoHo Artists Legalized" pie="February 10, 1971 issue of the SoHo Artists Association newsletter announcing “SoHo Artists Legalized”" /%}

Within a few years, owners could sell their spaces for substantially more than they had paid. Shapiro paid $5,000 for shares and initial improvements to the third-floor-rear loft, then sold it for $36,000 in 1973. Neighbor Judy Reichler remembered the sale as a revelation that there was money to be made.{% ref n=8 /%}

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-17.jpg" alt="Cover of a sale brochure by S.W. Bird and Company for a seventh-floor cooperative loft at 80 Wooster" pie="Joseph Schlichter's seventh-floor loft sale brochure with photos and floor plan, ca. 1990" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-18.jpg" alt="Page of small photographs of the loft interior from the sale brochure" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-19.jpg" alt="Hand-drawn floor plan and handwritten notes from the sale brochure" /%}
{% /galeria %}

For tenants trying to become owners, rising prices could close opportunities. Watts rented his fifth-floor loft to artist and teacher Ely Raman and his then-wife, Jeanie Black, in 1968, asking them to look after the Fluxus works stored there while he was away. When Black asked to buy the space, Watts agreed to $11,000. By the time the couple had gathered the money, his asking price had doubled and was already beyond their means.{% ref n=9 /%} Their participation in the building's artistic life, down to caring for its artworks, gave them no claim on the property's appreciation.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-20.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of a long, nearly empty loft with cast-iron columns and a worktable" pie="Studio area of Robert Watts' loft, ca. 1967" /%}

Mekas's sale helped finance a larger home for experimental film. By the late 1970s, Anthology Film Archives, which had moved into the former Cinematheque space in 1974, was outgrowing its Wooster premises. The collection needed more room, noise from the apartments above disrupted screenings, and meeting city building and fire codes required further investment.{% ref n=10 /%} Selling his Wooster space for $280,000—a net profit of $230,000—helped finance Anthology's relocation to a former courthouse at 32 Second Avenue. Extensive fundraising was still required, but the sale contributed to its larger home with two theaters and space for the institution's expanding activities.{% ref n=11 /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-21.jpg" alt="Black-and-white group photograph of Anthology Film Archives staff on the sidewalk in front of 80 Wooster Street" pie="Anthology Archives staff in front of 80 Wooster Street, ca. 1980" /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-22.jpg" alt="Black-and-white photograph of the ground-floor facade of 80 Wooster Street with parked cars in front" pie="Facade of 80 Wooster (Entrance to Jonas Mekas' Anthology Film Archives), ca. 1979" /%}

The residents upstairs could also have acquired the vacated ground floor, but the cooperative declined. Money was scarce, and members disagreed over whether making money belonged among the cooperative's purposes.{% ref n=12 /%} Gallery owner George Shechtman acquired the space instead.{% ref n=13 /%} By 2000, the fashion boutique Kenzo occupied the ground floor as SoHo's galleries increasingly left for cheaper premises in Chelsea and other neighborhoods. To secure the cooperative's permission to sublet, Shechtman agreed to pay the cooperative an additional fee averaging 15 percent of the difference between the rent he collected and his maintenance charges over the ten-year lease. These payments helped fund major building improvements, and residents came to benefit financially from the retail transformation of the neighborhood they helped establish.{% ref n=14 /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-23.png" alt="Two floor plans of the first floor of 80 Wooster Street, the 1970 layout as a theater with 70 seats and the 2010 layout as a retail store, with the labels Theater and Retail Store highlighted" pie="1970 vs 2010 Floor plan: First Floor" /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-24.jpg" alt="Clothing store with cast-iron columns and a red back wall in the former Cinematheque space" pie="Christopher Fischer store in former Cinematheque space, 2010" /%}

Shapiro's former loft was the same third-floor apartment offered for just under $3 million in April 2021. Its real estate listing advertised the building as "SoHo's original co-op" and highlighted income from The RealReal, the luxury consignment retailer that opened downstairs in 2017.{% ref n=15 /%} The loft sold for $3.2 million.{% ref n=16 /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-25.jpg" alt="Dark green storefront of The RealReal on the ground floor of 80 Wooster Street, with passers-by on the sidewalk" pie="Exterior of The RealReal at 80 Wooster Street" /%}

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/jay-26.jpg" alt="Screenshot of the CityRealty listing page for 80 Wooster Street #3R, with the line Last sold on Aug 16, 2021 for $3,200,000 highlighted, and the apartment's floor plan" pie="80 Wooster Street #3R listing" /%}

The artists at 80 Wooster shared an address, but their claims on its increasing value depended on ownership. The community's artistic history became something later owners could sell along with their shares. In the listing, the artist remains part of the property's appeal, whether or not an artist can afford to buy.

